Fair value is about 18.4. That is the sum of the expected values of the three dice: 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.5. The no-trade at 10.6/26.2 tells me very little, so fair value stays near 18.4.

With informed and noise traders equally likely and both spreads about 6.5, the best half-spread is roughly 1.15–1.2 standard deviations, about 7.5. So I quote slightly tighter than last time, centred near 18.4.

{"bid": 11.0, "ask": 25.8}

Fair value with nothing revealed is 16. That is the sum of the three die means: die 1 is 5.75, die 2 is 5.0 and die 3 is 5.25. The two earlier no-trade quotes were symmetric around 16, so they tell us nothing and the fair value stays at 16. The settlement value has a standard deviation of about 6.6, the same as the noise traders' spread.

I compared the expected profit per side for different half-spreads. The gain is 0.8 × half-spread × the chance a noise trader crosses it. The loss is 0.2 × what an informed trader takes when the value lies beyond the quote. The result is best at a half-spread of roughly 0.9 standard deviations, about 6, and nearly flat between about 5 and 6.6. I'll quote a half-spread of 5.7 around 16.

{"bid": 10.3, "ask": 21.7}

Fair value is about 19.83 (the dice means are 8, 5.33 and 6.5), with a standard deviation of about 5.7. Last quote's no-trade carries little information, so fair value stays about 19.8.

I balance the profit from noise traders against the losses to informed traders, treating both valuations as normal around fair value with about the same spread. On that approximation, the best half-spread is about 1 standard deviation, or roughly 5.6–5.7.

{"bid": 14.2, "ask": 25.5}

Die 2 is 10, so the settlement value is 10 plus die 1. Die 1 averages 5, so the public fair value is 15. The possible settlement values are 12, 13, 14, 14, 16, 16, 17 and 18. Quote 3 drew no trade, so the history adds no information.

An informed counterparty can only trade with me if my quotes are inside the 12–18 range, and then only when I lose. So the best quotes sit outside that range and earn only from noise traders. Against noise traders, the expected profit on one side is half of (distance from 15) × P(noise exceeds that distance). With noise standard deviation 4.7, this peaks at a distance of about 3.6–3.7, worth roughly 0.40 per side. That gives a bid of 11.3 and an ask of 18.7.

{"bid": 11.3, "ask": 18.7}

Each card's expected value is 7, so with nothing revealed the fair value of the three-card sum is 21. The standard deviation of the sum, dealt without replacement, is about 6.35.

The noise traders' spread is similar (6.4). I set the half-spread to balance two effects: noise trades give me a profit, and informed trades cost me money. The best half-spread comes out at roughly 1.2 standard deviations, about 7.5 points either side of 21. That gives a bid of 13.5 and an ask of 28.5.

{"bid": 13.5, "ask": 28.5}

Expected sum = 6.33 (die 1) + 6.25 (die 2) + 6.33 (die 3) ≈ 18.9, with standard deviation ≈ 6.5. The distribution has two peaks, because die 2 is either low (1 or 2) or 11. That puts the peaks near 14 and near 24.

The first quote got no trade, which tells us little. I'll tighten from 9.5/28.5 but stay just outside both peaks. This limits losses to informed traders while still giving noise traders a reasonable chance to trade.

{"bid": 12.5, "ask": 25.5}

The prior expected sum of three cards is 21, with a standard deviation of about 6.35. The counterparty at quote 1 sold to me at 16.5. That trade is evidence the sum may be low, since informed traders only sell if the sum is below 16.5. Bayesian updating moves the public fair value down to about 18.9.

I am long 1, so I lean slightly lower and keep a fairly wide spread to protect against informed traders.

{"bid": 15.0, "ask": 23.0}

Fair value now: card 1 is a 2. The other 51 cards sum to 364 − 2 = 362, so each averages about 7.10. Two unknown cards add about 14.2, giving a fair value near **16.2**.

The two unknown cards have a standard deviation of about 5.2. That gives informed traders a real edge on wide moves, so I'm quoting about ±4.5 around fair value.

{"bid": 11.7, "ask": 20.7}

Card 1 is a 2, so before any trading the expected sum is 2 + 2 × (362/51) ≈ 16.2. Since then, counterparties have bought at my ask twice, at 20.7 and 21.8. That pattern is far more likely if the remaining two cards sum high (about 20 or more) than if they sum low. A rough Bayesian update puts about 70% weight on a high total near 23.5 and about 27% on a low total near 15. That gives a fair value of roughly 21.

I'm short 2, so I widen around 21 to guard against adverse selection. I also lean the quote slightly toward buying back.

{"bid": 19.0, "ask": 24.3}

The prior expected sum of three cards is 21, with a standard deviation of about 6.35. Someone bought at 27.4, which suggests a high value. An informed trader buys only if the sum is above 27.4, which has about a 15% chance and implies a mean near 30.8. A noise trader buys about 10% of the time. Weighting those gives a posterior fair value of roughly 24.3, with a heavy upper tail.

I'm short 1, so I'll keep a wide spread and lean slightly upward.

{"bid": 20.0, "ask": 29.5}

Fair value now is about 23.8: the mean card is 119/15 ≈ 7.93, so three cards sum to about 23.8, with a standard deviation of about 9.4. The quote at 13/35 got no trade, which tells us little.

Informed traders know the outcome and the sum is very spread out, so tight quotes lose money to them. Under a rough normal approximation, a half-spread of about 10 or more is needed to break even or make a small profit. A half-spread of 5 to 8 loses money. So I keep the quote wide, slightly tighter than the last one.

{"bid": 14, "ask": 34}

Fair value is 23.4, since the average card is 7.8 and three cards are dealt.

The sum has a standard deviation of about 7.2. An informed trader adds that much adverse selection, and noise traders are spread by 5.4. To cover this I'll use a fairly wide spread of about ±4.4 around fair value.

{"bid": 19.0, "ask": 27.8}

Fair value: the 13 cards sum to 53, so each card averages about 4.08. Three cards are dealt, so the expected total is about **12.2**.

Uncertainty: the dealt sum has a standard deviation of about 6. The distribution is right-skewed because of the two 12s. Noise traders' valuations spread around 12.2 with a standard deviation of 8.9. With only a 20% chance of an informed trader, I can quote a moderately wide spread around 12.2. That still gets noise trades and limits how much an informed trader can take from me.

{"bid": 8.7, "ask": 15.8}

Fair value: card 1 is 12, so 12 cards remain, worth 41 in total. That is 41/12 ≈ 3.42 per card on average. The expected settlement is 12 + 2 × 3.42 ≈ 18.8.

The earlier trades were one buy and one sell, and the last quote got no trade, so the history doesn't shift my estimate much. I'll quote roughly symmetrically around 18.8 with a spread of about ±3.5. That should cover the informed-trader risk while still catching noise traders, whose valuations spread widely (standard deviation 8.9).

{"bid": 15.4, "ask": 22.3}

Fair value: after flips 0 and 1, the chance the coin is the 0.8 coin is 0.064 / 0.214 ≈ 0.30. That gives P(heads) ≈ 0.59 for each future flip. Expected heads ≈ 1 + 6 × 0.59 ≈ 4.54, so fair value ≈ 22.7.

I'll quote symmetrically around 22.7 with a half-spread of about 3.1. That should still earn from noise traders while limiting losses to informed traders.

{"bid": 19.6, "ask": 25.8}

Given flip 1 = 0, the coin-only fair value is about 2.1. But the trades so far point lower. The last counterparty sold at a bid of 1. If they were informed, the settlement must be 0, so a Bayesian update on the trade history puts roughly 40% on a settlement of 0. That brings my fair value down to about 1.2–1.5.

A meaningful bid would mostly be hit by informed sellers when the settlement is 0, so I'll keep the bid at 0. I'm long one unit and would like to sell, so I'll post an ask a little above the public fair value to attract noise buyers.

{"bid": 0, "ask": 2.4}

Fair value with nothing revealed is 7 × E[p] = 7 × (0.3·0.2 + 0.4·0.5 + 0.3·0.7) = 7 × 0.47 = **3.29**. A quote with no trade tells us little, so fair value stays about 3.29.

Settlement varies widely, and informed traders (20% of arrivals) know it exactly. Noise traders' valuations have a standard deviation of 2.7, so a moderate spread still earns from them. The last quote got no trade, so I'll tighten slightly to about ±0.8 around fair value.

{"bid": 2.5, "ask": 4.1}

Expected P(heads) = 0.2·0.2 + 0.3·0.7 + 0.5·0.9 = 0.70, so the fair value is 5 flips × 0.7 × 5 = **17.5**.

The settlement value has a standard deviation of about 7.8. Most of that spread comes from not knowing which coin was picked. Informed traders could hurt me, so I'll quote about 4 points either side of fair. Noise traders, whose valuations spread by ±12, will still trade often at those prices.

{"bid": 13.5, "ask": 21.5}

Starting from a uniform prior over 0–10 (mean 5), I updated on the two trades. A buyer at 7.3 is more likely when the value is 8–10. A seller at 4 is more likely when the value is 0–3. Together they leave a bimodal posterior with mean about 4.95.

Those tails make adverse selection from informed traders costly. Checking expected profit per quote, an ask near 7.3 and a bid near 2.6 come out best, at roughly +0.19 to +0.20 each.

{"bid": 2.6, "ask": 7.3}

Given the revealed draws (blue, blue, red), the chance of each red-ball count R is proportional to R(10−R)(9−R). That gives an expected R of 3.8, so fair value is about 38, with a standard deviation of about 18.

Noise traders are spread widely (standard deviation 47). The profit-maximising half-spread against them is roughly 0.75 × 47 ≈ 35. A spread that wide also keeps most informed trades away, since there is less than a 10% chance that R is 7 or more. I'm short 1, so I shade the quote slightly upward.

{"bid": 12, "ask": 68}

Posterior check: after two red draws, each possible red count R is weighted by R(R−1). That gives an expected red count of 8, so public fair value is about 80. Earlier trades add little information. The sale at 12 could only have been informed if R were 0 or 1, which the draws now rule out. The buy at 112 was above the maximum possible value of 100, so it must have been noise.

At ask 115, an informed trader never buys, since the value is at most 100. Noise buyers arrive with probability about 0.23 and pay 35 over fair, worth about 6.4 per quote. At bid 45, informed sellers can only hurt me in the low-value states, which are now unlikely, so that side is also worth about 6.3. My position is 0, so both sides are open.

{"bid": 45, "ask": 115}

Fair value is 4.5, since 0 to 9 red balls are equally likely and nothing has been revealed yet. The last quote, 0/9, got no trade, which tells us nothing new.

Expected profit on each side is the gain from noise traders minus the loss to informed traders. It peaks near 9 on the ask (about 8.9, worth roughly 0.34 per quote) and near 0 on the bid (about 0.1), by symmetry. Quoting much tighter loses too much to informed traders, because the settlement value is spread so widely.

